---
title: Remotes
summary: The wire layer. remotes(site) mints nav, doc, and search as Kit remotes — prerendered by default, live when you ask. The doc body bakes into a region ticket so the corpus stays server-side.
related:
  - pharos/pharos
  - pharos/search
  - content/collections
---

`site` is server-only — it holds the corpus. The page needs data. `remotes()` mints the crossing: the sidebar tree, one page's view, and search, as Kit remotes in a `.remote.ts`. The browser imports only these; the collection never follows.

> **When to use.** Always, for a pharos site. The remotes are the one sanctioned path from the server-side `site` to a page. Rendering the nav or a body without them means importing the collection into a client module — the exact leak the [server-module rule](/docs/content/collections) exists to prevent.

## Mint them

```ts
// src/lib/site.remote.ts
import { remotes } from 'ogygia/pharos/server';
import { site } from './site.server';

export const { nav, doc, search } = remotes(site, { base: '/docs' });
```

Three remotes, each a plain async function the page awaits:

- **`nav(slug?)`** — the sidebar tree, hrefs baked for `base`. On a [dimensioned](/docs/pharos/dimensions) site, pass the current slug so the tree reflects that coordinate.
- **`doc(slug)`** — one page's view: `data`, `meta`, `headings`, prev/next, and the **baked body**.
- **`search(q)`** — ranked hits from the [search](/docs/pharos/search) brain.

## The body crosses as a ticket

A body is a live [region](/docs/regions/held-regions) — a same-pass SSR render. It cannot be serialized as-is. `doc` **awaits** it, which bakes its SSR HTML into the region ticket: HTML-only, no source, no corpus. The page renders that with `<Region>` (via `<Doc>`), and any island the source imported wakes from the baked markup:

```svelte
<script>
  import { Doc } from 'ogygia/pharos';
  import { doc } from '$lib/site.remote';

  const view = (await doc(slug))!;   // view.entry.body is a baked ticket
</script>

<Doc {view} />
```

That is why the docs corpus can be megabytes yet a page ships only its own baked HTML.

## Prerender or live

By default `nav` and `doc` **prerender** — static pages, computed at build. Some sites want them dynamic (an authenticated preview, per-request content). The `modes` option picks per remote:

```ts
export const { nav, doc, search } = remotes(site, {
  base: '/docs',
  modes: { doc: 'query' }   // doc runs per request; nav still prerenders
});
```

- **`'prerender'`** (default for `nav`/`doc`) — baked at build, served static.
- **`'query'`** — runs on the server per request.

`search` is always a `query` — a search is inherently dynamic. (For a fully static search box, ship the [index](/docs/pharos/search) instead and query it in a client worker; then you don't mint `search` at all.)

## Prerender entries

The route still needs to know which slugs to bake. `site.entries()` is every leaf in the outline, including declared old addresses so redirect stubs bake too:

```ts
// src/routes/docs/[...slug]/+page.server.ts
import { site } from '$lib/site.server';

export const prerender = true;
export const entries = site.entries;
export const load = site.load;   // the 404 guard + alias redirects
```

## Do / don't

- **Do** mint remotes in a `.remote.ts` and import only those from pages. That is the whole corpus-safety story.
- **Do** let `doc` bake the body. Awaiting it is what turns a live region into something that can cross the wire.
- **Don't** try to return a raw `entry.body` from a remote or a server load — it is a same-pass render, and ogygia stops you with an error saying exactly that.
- **Don't** reach for `query` mode by default. Prerendered pages are faster and cheaper; switch a remote to `query` only when the content genuinely varies per request.
